\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For $n\in\mathbb N$, let $\sigma(n)=\sum_{d\mid n,\ d>0}d$ and $s(n)=\sigma(n)-n$. Distinct positive integers $m,n$ form a betrothed pair when $s(m)=n+1$ and $s(n)=m+1$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Are there infinitely many unordered betrothed pairs $\{m,n\}$? Equivalently, are infinitely many positive integers members of such pairs? \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Are there infinitely many betrothed pairs?

